<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-col-row-null-space-test-el-colspace">
<title> Determining membership in column space </title>

<introduction><p>
	In each of the following,
	determine whether vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> is in the column space of matrix <m> A </m>.
	In those cases where it is,
	express <m> \uvec{v} </m> as a linear combination of the columns of <m> A </m>.
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement>

		<p>
			<m>
				A =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					-1 \amp  7 \amp  3 \\
					-1 \amp  6 \amp  3 \\
					 2 \amp -8 \amp -5
				\end{abmatrix}
			</m>;
			<m> \uvec{v} = \begin{abmatrix}{r} 4 \\ 3 \\ -4 \end{abmatrix} </m>
		</p>

	</statement>

	<solution><p>
		Vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> is in the column space of <m> A </m> precisely when the system <m> A \uvec{x} = \uvec{v} </m> is consistent.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				-1 \amp  7 \amp  3 \amp  4 \\
				-1 \amp  6 \amp  3 \amp  3 \\
				 2 \amp -8 \amp -5 \amp -4
			\end{abmatrix}
			\qquad \rowredarrow \qquad
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp -3 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp  1 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp -2
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>
		Since the system is consistent,
		<m> \uvec{v} </m> is in the column space of <m> A </m>,
		with
		<md>
			\uvec{v}
			= (-3) \begin{abmatrix}{r} -1 \\ -1 \\  2 \end{abmatrix}
			+   1  \begin{abmatrix}{r}  7 \\  6 \\ -8 \end{abmatrix}
			+ (-2) \begin{abmatrix}{r}  3 \\  3 \\ -5 \end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement>

		<p>
			<m>
				A =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					 1 \amp -1 \amp  3 \\
					 3 \amp -2 \amp  6 \\
					 2 \amp -2 \amp  7 \\
					-1 \amp  0 \amp -3
				\end{abmatrix}
			</m>;
			<m> \uvec{v} = \begin{abmatrix}{r} 3 \\ 1 \\ 8 \\ 0 \end{abmatrix} </m>
		</p>

	</statement>

	<solution><p>
		Vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> is in the column space of <m> A </m> precisely when the system <m> A \uvec{x} = \uvec{v} </m> is consistent.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				 1 \amp -1 \amp  3 \amp 3 \\
				 3 \amp -2 \amp  6 \amp 1 \\
				 2 \amp -2 \amp  7 \amp 8 \\
				-1 \amp  0 \amp -3 \amp 0
			\end{abmatrix}
			\qquad \rowredarrow \qquad
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \\
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>
		Since the system is inconsistent,
		<m> \uvec{v} </m> is <em> not </em> in the column space of <m> A </m>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement>

		<p>
			<m>
				A =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					 1 \amp  1 \amp  6 \\
					 0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \\
					-1 \amp  1 \amp -4 \\
					 1 \amp  3 \amp  8
				\end{abmatrix}
			</m>;
			<m> \uvec{v} = \begin{abmatrix}{r} 1 \\ -4 \\ 7 \\ 9 \end{abmatrix} </m>
		</p>

	</statement>

	<solution><p>
		Vector <m> \uvec{v} </m> is in the column space of <m> A </m> precisely when the system <m> A \uvec{x} = \uvec{v} </m> is consistent.
		<md>
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				 1 \amp  1 \amp  6 \amp  1 \\
				 0 \amp -1 \amp -1 \amp -4 \\
				-1 \amp  1 \amp -4 \amp  7 \\
				 1 \amp  3 \amp  8 \amp  9
			\end{abmatrix}
			\qquad \rowredarrow \qquad
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
				1 \amp 0 \amp 5 \amp -3 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp  4 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</md>
		Since the system is consistent,
		<m> \uvec{v} </m> is in the column space of <m> A </m>,
		with
		<md>
			\uvec{v}
			= (-3) \begin{abmatrix}{r} 1 \\  0 \\ -1 \\ 1 \end{abmatrix}
			+   4  \begin{abmatrix}{r} 1 \\ -1 \\  1 \\ 3 \end{abmatrix}
			+   0  \begin{abmatrix}{r} 6 \\ -1 \\ -4 \\ 8 \end{abmatrix}
		</md>.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
